## Overview
`java.util.Scanner` provides interactive console input by parsing primitive types and strings from `System.in` using regular expression delimiters.

---

## Initialization & Syntax

```java
import java.util.Scanner;

Scanner scanner = new Scanner(System.in);
```

---

## Key Methods Reference

| Method | Return Type | Delimiter | Consumes Trailing Newline (`\n`)? | Thrown Exception on Invalid Input |
| :--- | :--- | :--- | :---: | :--- |
| **`scanner.nextLine()`** | `String` | Line break (`\n`) | **Yes** | `NoSuchElementException` |
| **`scanner.next()`** | `String` | Whitespace | No | `NoSuchElementException` |
| **`scanner.nextInt()`** | `int` | Whitespace | No | `InputMismatchException` |
| **`scanner.nextDouble()`**| `double` | Whitespace | No | `InputMismatchException` |
| **`scanner.nextBoolean()`**| `boolean`| Whitespace | No | `InputMismatchException` |
| **`scanner.hasNextInt()`** | `boolean` | Lookahead token | — | None (safe guard) |

---

## The Trailing Newline Buffer Trap

Calling `scanner.nextInt()` reads only numeric characters and leaves the trailing newline character (`\n`) in the input buffer. When a subsequent `scanner.nextLine()` runs, it immediately encounters that leftover `\n`, consumes it, and returns an empty string without waiting for user input.

### Problem Code
```java
int age = scanner.nextInt();      // Leaves '\n' in buffer
String name = scanner.nextLine(); // Immediately consumes '\n', returning ""!
```

### Production Best Practice Fix
Never mix token-based reading (`nextInt`) with line-based reading (`nextLine`). Always read entire lines with `nextLine()` and parse explicitly:

```java
System.out.print("Enter age: ");
int age = Integer.parseInt(scanner.nextLine().trim());

System.out.print("Enter name: ");
String name = scanner.nextLine(); // Buffer is clean and safe
```

---

## Safe Input Validation Guard

To prevent `InputMismatchException` from crashing the application when users type invalid input:

```java
int validPort = -1;
while (validPort < 0) {
    System.out.print("Enter port (1024-65535): ");
    if (scanner.hasNextInt()) {
        int input = scanner.nextInt();
        scanner.nextLine(); // Flush newline
        if (input >= 1024 && input <= 65535) {
            validPort = input;
        } else {
            System.out.println("Error: Port out of range.");
        }
    } else {
        System.out.println("Error: Numeric digits required.");
        scanner.nextLine(); // Discard invalid token
    }
}
```

---

## Pitfalls

- **Closing `Scanner(System.in)` in Helper Methods:** Calling `scanner.close()` permanently closes the underlying JVM `System.in` stream. Close the scanner only once upon application shutdown.
- **Unhandled Exceptions:** Direct calls like `scanner.nextInt()` throw unchecked `InputMismatchException` if non-numeric characters are entered.
- **Locale-Dependent Decimals:** `scanner.nextDouble()` expects either a comma `,` or period `.` depending on the host OS default locale (`Locale.getDefault()`).

---

## Example

```java
import java.util.Scanner;

public class ConsoleInputDemo {
    public static void main(String[] args) {
        // Simulating user input stream for verification
        String mockInput = "8080\nAuthService\n";
        Scanner scanner = new Scanner(mockInput);

        System.out.println("Reading configuration...");
        int port = Integer.parseInt(scanner.nextLine().trim());
        String serviceName = scanner.nextLine().trim();

        System.out.printf("Service '%s' configured on port %d.%n", serviceName, port);
        scanner.close();
    }
}
```

<details data-type="output">
<summary>View Output</summary>

```text
Reading configuration...
Service 'AuthService' configured on port 8080.
```
</details>

---

## Practice

### Q: Why does calling `scanner.close()` inside a method prevent any subsequent `Scanner` on `System.in` from working?
<details data-type="solution">
<summary>Reveal Answer</summary>

> **Answer:** `Scanner.close()` closes the underlying `Readable` or `InputStream` source it wraps. Closing `System.in` closes standard input at the OS/JVM process level; it cannot be reopened during the application lifecycle.
</details>

## Overview
Conditional statements in Java divert execution flow at runtime based on the evaluation of boolean expressions. Unlike dynamically typed languages, Java strictly requires condition expressions to resolve to a primitive `boolean` or `Boolean` wrapper; numbers and objects cannot serve as truthy or falsy values.

---

## Syntax & Branching Architecture

```
                       [ Evaluate Condition ]
                                 │
                 ┌───────────────┴───────────────┐
             ( true )                        ( false )
                 │                               │
                 ▼                               ▼
         [ Execute Branch ]              [ Next Condition / else ]
                 │                               │
                 └───────────────┬───────────────┘
                                 ▼
                     [ Continue Execution ]
```

### Statement Formats

```java
// 1. Single Branch
if (condition) {
    // Executes only if condition is true
}

// 2. Dual Branch
if (condition) {
    // Primary path
} else {
    // Alternative path
}

// 3. Multi-Branch Ladder (Mutually Exclusive)
if (conditionA) {
    // Branch A
} else if (conditionB) {
    // Branch B (checked only if conditionA is false)
} else {
    // Fallback branch
}

// 4. Ternary Conditional Expression
variable = (condition) ? valueIfTrue : valueIfFalse;
```

---

## Key Characteristics & Evaluation Rules

| Feature | Rule / Behavior | Code Example |
| :--- | :--- | :--- |
| **Type Constraint** | Expression must strictly evaluate to `boolean` | `if (status == 1)` valid; `if (status)` invalid |
| **Short-Circuit Logic** | Left-to-right evaluation terminates as soon as outcome is certain | `if (user != null && user.isActive())` prevents NPE |
| **Block Scope** | Variables declared inside braces `{}` exist only within that branch | `{ int temp = 10; } // temp unreachable outside` |
| **Ternary Typing** | Both branches of `? :` must conform to a common target type | `Number n = flag ? 1 : 2.5; // Autoboxed to Double` |

---

## Implementation Example

```java
public class TieredFeeCalculator {
    public static void main(String[] args) {
        double transactionAmount = 1450.00;
        boolean isEnterpriseClient = true;
        double processingFeeRate;

        // Mutually exclusive boundary evaluation
        if (transactionAmount >= 5000.0) {
            processingFeeRate = 0.010; // 1.0%
        } else if (transactionAmount >= 1000.0) {
            processingFeeRate = isEnterpriseClient ? 0.012 : 0.018; // 1.2% vs 1.8%
        } else {
            processingFeeRate = 0.025; // 2.5%
        }

        double fee = transactionAmount * processingFeeRate;
        double netSettlement = transactionAmount - fee;

        System.out.printf("Gross Amount  : $%,10.2f%n", transactionAmount);
        System.out.printf("Fee Rate      : %10.2f%%%n", processingFeeRate * 100.0);
        System.out.printf("Processing Fee: $%,10.2f%n", fee);
        System.out.printf("Net Settlement: $%,10.2f%n", netSettlement);
    }
}
```

### Execution Output:
```text
Gross Amount  : $  1,450.00
Fee Rate      :       1.20%
Processing Fee: $     17.40
Net Settlement: $  1,432.60
```

---

## Pitfalls

- **Assignment Instead of Comparison:** Writing `if (isValid = true)` accidentally assigns `true` to `isValid` and evaluates to `true`. With booleans, compare using `if (isValid)` directly rather than `if (isValid == true)`.
- **Dangling Else Trap:** Omitting curly braces binds an `else` to the closest preceding `if`, producing silent logic divergence:
  ```java
  // BUG: The else attaches to innerIf, not outerIf!
  if (outer)
      if (inner) doA();
  else doB();

  // FIX: Always use explicit curly braces
  if (outer) {
      if (inner) { doA(); }
  } else {
      doB();
  }
  ```
- **Null Reference Before Field Access:** Reversing operands in logical AND without null guarding throws `NullPointerException`:
  ```java
  // DANGEROUS: Throws NPE if customer is null
  if (customer.isVerified() && customer != null)
  // SAFE: Relies on short-circuit evaluation
  if (customer != null && customer.isVerified())
  ```
- **Overusing Nested Ternaries:** Chaining more than one ternary operator impairs maintainability and complicates code coverage analysis.

---

## Practice

### Question
Given the following method, what does `checkStatus(false, true)` print?
```java
public static void checkStatus(boolean flagA, boolean flagB) {
    if (flagA)
        if (flagB)
            System.out.print("1");
    else
        System.out.print("2");
    System.out.print("3");
}
```

### Explanation
1. Because braces `{}` are omitted, the `else` binds to the immediately preceding `if (flagB)`, not `if (flagA)`.
2. When `flagA` is `false`, the outer `if` condition fails entirely, skipping the inner `if-else` construct.
3. Execution proceeds immediately to the statement following the block: `System.out.print("3");`.
4. Output: `3`.

## Overview
A `switch` construct branches program execution by matching a selector expression against constant `case` labels. Starting in Java 14 (JEP 361), Java supports both traditional switch statements and modern switch expressions that return values directly using arrow syntax (`->`) and `yield`, eliminating fall-through vulnerabilities.

---

## Evolution & Comparison

```
   Classic Switch Statement (Java 1+)           Modern Switch Expression (Java 14+)
 ┌──────────────────────────────────────┐     ┌──────────────────────────────────────┐
 │ switch (status) {                    │     │ String desc = switch (status) {      │
 │     case 200:                        │     │     case 200, 201    -> "Success";   │
 │         res = "OK";                  │     │     case 400, 404    -> "Client Err";│
 │         break; // Fall-through risk! │     │     case 500         -> {            │
 │     default:                         │     │         log("500");                  │
 │         res = "Unknown";             │     │         yield "Server Err";          │
 │ }                                    │     │     }                                │
 └──────────────────────────────────────┘     │     default          -> "Unknown";   │
                                              │ }; // Expression ends with ';'       │
                                              └──────────────────────────────────────┘
```

### Classic Statement vs Modern Expression

| Property | Classic Statement (`:`) | Modern Expression (`->`) |
| :--- | :--- | :--- |
| **Execution Style** | Statement (does not yield a value directly) | Expression (evaluates and returns a value) |
| **Fall-Through** | Yes: continues into next case unless stopped by `break` | No: arrow syntax executes exactly one matched branch |
| **Multiple Labels** | Stacked labels (`case 1: case 2:`) | Comma-separated (`case 1, 2 ->`) |
| **Exhaustiveness** | Optional (unmatched input skips switch) | Mandatory (must cover all cases or provide `default`) |
| **Block Return** | N/A | Handled via `yield value;` |
| **Terminating `;`** | Closing brace only (`}`) | Semicolon required after closing brace (`};`) |

---

## Permitted Selector Types & Bytecode Architecture

A `switch` selector must evaluate to:
- Primitive integer types: `byte`, `short`, `char`, `int` (and their boxed wrappers `Byte`, `Short`, `Character`, `Integer`).
- `java.lang.String` (Java 7+).
- Enumerations (`enum`, Java 5+).
- **Prohibited Types:** `long`, `float`, `double`, `boolean`.

### JVM Bytecode Dispatch

The Java compiler compiles switch constructs into one of two bytecode instructions:
1. **`tableswitch`:** Applied when case values are dense/contiguous integers. Uses direct $O(1)$ indexed jump table array lookup.
2. **`lookupswitch`:** Applied when case values are sparse or strings (using `hashCode()`). Uses binary search across a sorted key table in $O(\log N)$ time.

---

## Implementation Example

```java
public class HttpStatusDispatcher {
    public enum Environment { DEV, STAGING, PROD }

    public static String resolveEndpointConfig(int statusCode, Environment env) {
        // Modern Switch Expression returning a value directly
        String category = switch (statusCode) {
            case 200, 201, 204 -> "SUCCESS";
            case 301, 302, 307, 308 -> "REDIRECTION";
            case 400, 401, 403, 404 -> "CLIENT_ERROR";
            case 500, 502, 503, 504 -> {
                // Multi-line block utilizing yield
                String alert = String.format("CRITICAL [%s]: Server failure code %d", env, statusCode);
                System.err.println(alert);
                yield "SERVER_ERROR";
            }
            default -> "UNRECOGNIZED_STATUS";
        };

        return category;
    }

    public static void main(String[] args) {
        String result1 = resolveEndpointConfig(200, Environment.PROD);
        String result2 = resolveEndpointConfig(503, Environment.PROD);

        System.out.printf("Code 200 Category: %s%n", result1);
        System.out.printf("Code 503 Category: %s%n", result2);
    }
}
```

### Execution Output:
```text
Code 200 Category: SUCCESS
CRITICAL [PROD]: Server failure code 503
Code 503 Category: SERVER_ERROR
```

---

## Pitfalls

- **Accidental Fall-Through in Classic Switch:** Omitting `break` in classic colon syntax causes execution to bleed into subsequent cases regardless of their condition. Always prefer arrow syntax (`->`) in modern code.
- **Mixing Arrow (`->`) and Colon (`:`) Syntax:** Mixing syntax within the same switch block (e.g., `case 1 -> "A"; case 2: return "B";`) triggers a compiler error.
- **Non-Constant Expressions in `case` Labels:** Labels must be compile-time constants (literals or `final` primitive variables initialized at declaration). Non-final variables or runtime expressions are rejected.
- **Missing Exhaustiveness in Switch Expressions:** When using switch as an expression assigned to a variable, omitting `default` (or omitting cases when switching over an enum) fails compilation with `the switch expression does not cover all possible input values`.
- **Switching on `null`:** In classic switch and standard modern switch, passing a `null` `String`, `Integer`, or `enum` throws `NullPointerException` before checking any cases (unless Java 21 Pattern Matching with explicit `case null ->` is used).

---

## Practice

### Question
Given the following code, what value is assigned to `result`?
```java
int day = 2;
String result = "";
switch (day) {
    case 1: result += "Mon";
    case 2: result += "Tue";
    case 3: result += "Wed";
        break;
    case 4: result += "Thu";
    default: result += "Def";
}
```

### Explanation
1. Execution matches `case 2:`, appending `"Tue"` to `result`.
2. Because `case 2:` lacks a `break` statement, execution falls through unconditionally into `case 3:`, appending `"Wed"`.
3. `case 3:` contains a `break;`, which terminates the switch statement and skips `case 4:` and `default`.
4. Result: `"TueWed"`.

## Overview
Loops control repetitive code execution based on evaluation conditions. Java provides four loop structures: counter-controlled `for`, pre-conditioned `while`, post-conditioned `do-while` (guaranteed at least one execution), and enhanced `for-each` which relies on `java.lang.Iterable` or array length.

---

## Loop Comparison & Execution Lifecycle

```
    Standard for / while (Pre-Test)                 do-while (Post-Test)
 ┌──────────────────────────────────┐        ┌──────────────────────────────────┐
 │  [ Evaluate Condition ]          │        │  [ Execute Loop Body ]           │
 │       │                          │        │       │                          │
 │  (true) ──► [ Execute Body ]     │        │       ▼                          │
 │       │            │             │        │  [ Evaluate Condition ]          │
 │       │     [ Step Update ]      │        │       │                          │
 │       │            │             │        │  (true) ──► (Repeat Body)        │
 │       └────────────┘             │        │  (false) ─► [ Exit Loop ]        │
 │  (false) ─► [ Exit Loop ]        │        └──────────────────────────────────┘
 └──────────────────────────────────┘
```

| Loop Construct | Condition Check Point | Minimum Runs | Primary Use Case |
| :--- | :--- | :--- | :--- |
| **`for (init; cond; update)`** | Pre-test | 0 | Known number of iterations, array index manipulation |
| **`for (T item : iterable)`** | Pre-test (via Iterator/Index) | 0 | Read-only sequence traversal without index counters |
| **`while (condition)`** | Pre-test | 0 | Indeterminate iterations dependent on dynamic state |
| **`do { ... } while (cond);`** | Post-test | 1 | Input polling, handshake retries, token renewal |

---

## Interruption & Labeled Jump Control

- **`break`:** Terminates the innermost enclosing loop or switch immediately.
- **`continue`:** Discards the rest of the current iteration, triggering the loop update/condition evaluation.
- **Labeled Loops (`labelName:`):** Provides a jump target for `break` or `continue` to escape multiple nested loop levels without flag variables:

```java
matrixSearch:
for (int i = 0; i < matrix.length; i++) {
    for (int j = 0; j < matrix[i].length; j++) {
        if (matrix[i][j] == target) {
            foundRow = i;
            foundCol = j;
            break matrixSearch; // Exits BOTH nested loops directly
        }
    }
}
```

---

## Implementation Example

```java
public class RetryAndSearchPipeline {
    public static void main(String[] args) {
        int[] sensorReadings = {42, -1, 105, -999, 88, 12};
        int errorSentinel = -999;
        int validSum = 0;
        int validCount = 0;

        // 1. Array traversal with continue and break
        for (int reading : sensorReadings) {
            if (reading == errorSentinel) {
                System.out.println("Encountered fatal sentinel value. Aborting analysis.");
                break;
            }
            if (reading < 0) {
                // Skip negative/invalid transient noise
                continue;
            }
            validSum += reading;
            validCount++;
        }

        double average = (validCount > 0) ? (double) validSum / validCount : 0.0;
        System.out.printf("Processed %d valid readings. Average: %.2f%n", validCount, average);

        // 2. Exponential backoff simulation via do-while
        int retryCount = 0;
        int maxRetries = 3;
        boolean connectionEstablished = false;

        do {
            retryCount++;
            System.out.printf("Attempting connection: attempt %d/%d...%n", retryCount, maxRetries);
            if (retryCount == 2) {
                connectionEstablished = true;
            }
        } while (!connectionEstablished && retryCount < maxRetries);

        System.out.printf("Connection status: %s (after %d attempts)%n",
                connectionEstablished ? "CONNECTED" : "FAILED", retryCount);
    }
}
```

### Execution Output:
```text
Processed 2 valid readings. Average: 73.50
Encountered fatal sentinel value. Aborting analysis.
Attempting connection: attempt 1/3...
Attempting connection: attempt 2/3...
Connection status: CONNECTED (after 2 attempts)
```

---

## Pitfalls

- **Off-By-One Indexing:** Using `<=` instead of `<` when indexing arrays (e.g., `for (int i = 0; i <= arr.length; i++)`) triggers `ArrayIndexOutOfBoundsException` on the terminal iteration.
- **Accidental Semicolon Placement:** Placing a semicolon immediately after a loop header (e.g., `while (condition); { body; }`) creates an empty statement body, resulting in an immediate infinite loop or unexpected single execution.
- **Modifying Collections During Enhanced `for`:** Adding or removing elements from a `Collection` inside a `for-each` loop invalidates its internal cursor and throws `ConcurrentModificationException`. Use `Iterator.remove()` or `Collection.removeIf()`.
- **Floating-Point Counters:** Using `float` or `double` as counter variables (e.g., `for (double d = 0.1; d <= 1.0; d += 0.1)`) creates rounding accumulation errors that prevent exact termination matches. Always use integer step counters.

---

## Practice

### Question
What is the final value of `count` after the following nested loop terminates?
```java
int count = 0;
outer:
for (int i = 0; i < 3; i++) {
    for (int j = 0; j < 3; j++) {
        if (i == 1 && j == 1) {
            continue outer;
        }
        count++;
    }
}
```

### Explanation
1. When `i = 0`: inner loop runs for `j = 0, 1, 2` $\rightarrow$ `count` increments 3 times (total: 3).
2. When `i = 1`:
   - `j = 0`: `count` increments to 4.
   - `j = 1`: `continue outer;` triggers, skipping the rest of the inner loop (`j = 2` never executes) and advancing directly to `i = 2`.
3. When `i = 2`: inner loop runs for `j = 0, 1, 2` $\rightarrow$ `count` increments 3 times (total: 7).
4. Final value of `count`: `7`.

## Overview
A method is a discrete, callable subroutine within a class that encapsulates business operations, receives arguments, and yields a typed result or `void`. Java enforces a strict **Pass-by-Value** evaluation strategy across all method invocations without exception.

---

## Anatomy & Call Stack Mechanics

```java
// Method Signature: calculateYield(double, int)
public static double calculateYield(double principal, int termMonths) {
    if (principal <= 0.0) return 0.0;
    return principal * Math.pow(1.0 + (0.05 / 12), termMonths);
}
```

```
                          JVM Call Stack Frame
 ┌────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┐
 │ [Frame 2: calculateYield]                              │
 │   - Local Variables: principal=1000.0, termMonths=12   │
 │   - Operand Stack: intermediate math calculations      │
 │   - Return Address: caller in main()                   │
 ├────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┤
 │ [Frame 1: main]                                        │
 │   - Local Variables: args, initialBalance, finalYield  │
 │   - Current Execution Pointer                          │
 └────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┘
  * Invocations push new stack frames; returning pops and reclaims frame memory.
```

---

## The Pass-by-Value Invariant

Java never passes variables by reference. It evaluates arguments and copies their literal values into the receiving method's parameter slots:

| Argument Category | Value Copied into Parameter | Effect of Reassignment Inside Method | Effect of State Mutation Inside Method |
| :--- | :--- | :--- | :--- |
| **Primitives** (`int`, `double`, etc.) | Literal binary bit-pattern | Zero effect on original caller variable | N/A (primitives have no internal state) |
| **Object References** | Literal memory pointer address | Zero effect on original caller reference | Mutates the single shared object in heap! |

```java
// Modifying internal state vs Reassigning pointer
void modify(int primitiveVal, List<String> listRef) {
    primitiveVal = 50;                  // Caller's int unchanged
    listRef.add("Added");               // Caller sees modified heap object
    listRef = new ArrayList<>();       // Reassignment: local parameter points elsewhere; caller unchanged!
}
```

---

## Implementation Example

```java
import java.util.ArrayList;
import java.util.List;

public class ParameterPassingDemonstration {
    public static void compute(int scalar, List<String> buffer) {
        scalar += 100;                 // Modifies local frame copy only
        buffer.add("Event-Recorded");  // Mutates shared heap object
        buffer = new ArrayList<>();    // Rebinds local variable to new object
        buffer.add("Orphaned-Event");  // Does not affect caller
    }

    public static void main(String[] args) {
        int originalScalar = 25;
        List<String> originalBuffer = new ArrayList<>();
        originalBuffer.add("Initial-State");

        compute(originalScalar, originalBuffer);

        System.out.printf("Scalar value after call: %d%n", originalScalar);
        System.out.printf("Buffer size after call : %d%n", originalBuffer.size());
        System.out.printf("Buffer contents         : %s%n", originalBuffer);
    }
}
```

### Execution Output:
```text
Scalar value after call: 25
Buffer size after call : 2
Buffer contents         : [Initial-State, Event-Recorded]
```

---

## Pitfalls

- **The "Pass-by-Reference" Myth:** Thinking that passing an object passes the variable itself. If you assign `param = new OtherObject();` inside a method, the caller's variable still points to the original object.
- **Missing Return Path in Conditional Branches:** Every code branch in a non-void method must explicitly execute a `return` statement or throw an exception. Omitting a return in an `else` branch triggers compiler error `missing return statement`.
- **Unbounded Recursion:** Methods calling themselves without an attainable base condition exhaust the thread call stack, throwing `java.lang.StackOverflowError`.
- **Primitive Autoboxing Overhead in Signatures:** Declaring return types as wrapper objects (e.g. `Double` instead of `double`) introduces unnecessary heap allocation and risks unexpected `NullPointerException` during implicit unboxing.

---

## Practice

### Question
Given the following snippet, what does `main` print?
```java
public class PassTest {
    static void swap(StringBuilder x, StringBuilder y) {
        x.append("-A");
        y = x;
        y.append("-B");
    }
    public static void main(String[] args) {
        StringBuilder s1 = new StringBuilder("1");
        StringBuilder s2 = new StringBuilder("2");
        swap(s1, s2);
        System.out.println(s1 + " " + s2);
    }
}
```

### Explanation
1. `swap` receives copies of the references `s1` and `s2`.
2. `x.append("-A")` mutates the heap object referenced by `s1` (`"1-A"`).
3. `y = x` reassigns the local parameter `y` to point to the same object as `x` (`s1`). The caller's `s2` reference remains completely untouched.
4. `y.append("-B")` mutates that same heap object, making `s1` `"1-A-B"`.
5. `s2` remained pointing to `"2"`.
6. Output: `1-A-B 2`.

## Overview
Method overloading is compile-time (static) polymorphism where multiple methods share the same identifier with distinct parameter lists (count, types, or order). Variable-length arguments (varargs, `Type...`) enable methods to accept zero or more arguments, which the compiler compiles into an array parameter.

---

## Method Signature & Resolution Hierarchy

A method signature comprises solely the **Method Name** and **Parameter Type List**. Return type, access modifiers, and `throws` clauses are NOT part of the signature. Overloading by changing only the return type causes a compilation error (`method already defined`).

### The 4-Phase Resolution Sequence

When matching an invocation to overloaded candidates, the compiler resolves in strict four-phase order:

```
 Invocation ──► [ Phase 1: Exact Match / Widening without Boxing ]
                       │ (if no match)
                       ▼
                [ Phase 2: Widening to Larger Primitive Type ]
                       │ (if no match)
                       ▼
                [ Phase 3: Autoboxing / Unboxing ]
                       │ (if no match)
                       ▼
                [ Phase 4: Varargs Array Packing (...) ]
```

| Resolution Step | Candidate Signature | Invocation `test(5)` Matches? | Reason |
| :--- | :--- | :--- | :--- |
| **1. Exact Match** | `test(int x)` | **Selected** (Phase 1) | Direct primitive match |
| **2. Widening** | `test(long x)` | Selected if `int` absent | Primitive promotion (`int` $\rightarrow$ `long`) |
| **3. Boxing** | `test(Integer x)` | Selected if 1 & 2 absent | Autoboxing (`int` $\rightarrow$ `Integer`) |
| **4. Varargs** | `test(int... x)` | Selected only as last resort | Lowest precedence in resolution |

---

## Varargs Syntax Rules

1. **Terminal Parameter Only:** A method can declare at most one varargs parameter, and it **must** be the final parameter in the signature.
   ```java
   void log(String tag, int level, String... messages) // VALID
   void bad(String... messages, int level)            // COMPILER ERROR
   ```
2. **Implicit Array Allocation:** Calling `process("a", "b")` causes the compiler to generate `process(new String[]{"a", "b"})`. Inside the method body, the parameter behaves strictly as `String[]`.

---

## Implementation Example

```java
public class QueryBuilderUtility {
    // 1. Single-argument base method
    public static String select(String table) {
        return "SELECT * FROM " + table;
    }

    // 2. Overloaded method with explicit single column
    public static String select(String table, String column) {
        return String.format("SELECT %s FROM %s", column, table);
    }

    // 3. Overloaded method with varargs for multi-column projection
    public static String select(String table, String firstCol, String... additionalCols) {
        StringBuilder sql = new StringBuilder("SELECT ").append(firstCol);
        for (String col : additionalCols) {
            sql.append(", ").append(col);
        }
        sql.append(" FROM ").append(table);
        return sql.toString();
    }

    public static void main(String[] args) {
        System.out.println(select("users"));
        System.out.println(select("users", "id"));
        System.out.println(select("users", "id", "email", "created_at"));
    }
}
```

### Execution Output:
```text
SELECT * FROM users
SELECT id FROM users
SELECT id, email, created_at FROM users
```

