import jakarta.servlet.http.HttpServlet;
import jakarta.servlet.http.HttpServletRequest;
import jakarta.servlet.http.HttpServletResponse;
import jakarta.servlet.http.HttpSession;
import java.io.IOException;

@WebServlet("/auth/session")
public class SecureSessionServlet extends HttpServlet {

    @Override
    protected void doPost(HttpServletRequest req, HttpServletResponse resp) throws IOException {
        // Invalidate old session to prevent Session Fixation attacks
        HttpSession oldSession = req.getSession(false);
        if (oldSession != null) {
            oldSession.invalidate();
        }

        // Create new clean session
        HttpSession newSession = req.getSession(true);
        newSession.setMaxInactiveInterval(1800); // 30 minutes timeout
        newSession.setAttribute("authenticatedUser", "engineer_01");

        // Manually set an application tracking cookie with modern security attributes
        Cookie userPref = new Cookie("theme", "dark");
        userPref.setHttpOnly(true);
        userPref.setSecure(true);
        userPref.setPath(req.getContextPath() + "/");
        userPref.setMaxAge(86400 * 30); // 30 days
        userPref.setAttribute("SameSite", "Lax");

        resp.addCookie(userPref);
        resp.getWriter().println("Session created and secured.");
    }
}
```

---

## Common Pitfalls & Security Traps

- **Session Fixation Vulnerability:**
  Keeping an existing session active across authentication boundaries allows an attacker who pre-planted a `JSESSIONID` to hijack the session. Always invoke `session.invalidate()` and create a new session upon successful login.
- **Storing Non-Serializable Objects in Distributed Environments:**
  In clustered environments with Tomcat session replication or Redis persistence, any object stored in `HttpSession` via `setAttribute()` must implement `java.io.Serializable`, otherwise serialization fails with `NotSerializableException`.
- **Calling `req.getSession()` Unnecessarily:**
  Invoking `req.getSession()` or `req.getSession(true)` on unauthenticated public endpoints (e.g., search, health check) forces the container to allocate a session and issue `Set-Cookie` for every bot/crawler, causing massive memory consumption. Use `req.getSession(false)` for read-only checks.

---

## Verification & Practice

**Question:** What is the critical behavioral difference between `request.getSession(true)` and `request.getSession(false)`?

**Explanation:** `request.getSession(true)` looks up the existing session associated with the request's `JSESSIONID`; if none exists, it creates and returns a brand-new `HttpSession`. `request.getSession(false)` returns the existing session if one is active, or returns `null` without creating a new session.

## Overview
Servlet Filters implement the Intercepting Filter pattern, inspecting, transforming, or blocking HTTP requests and responses before and after they reach target servlets or static resources. Configured in a chain via `FilterChain.doFilter()`, filters encapsulate cross-cutting concerns including authentication, compression, logging, and security headers.

---

## Filter Chain Execution Pipeline

```
Client Request
      │
      ▼
┌───────────────┐
│ Filter 1 (In) │  (e.g., Logging & Correlation ID)
└───────┬───────┘
        ▼
┌───────────────┐
│ Filter 2 (In) │  (e.g., Security & Authentication)
└───────┬───────┘
        ▼
┌───────────────┐
│ TargetServlet │  (Executes business logic: doGet / doPost)
└───────┬───────┘
        ▼
┌───────────────┐
│ Filter 2 (Out)│  (Response headers, timing calculation)
└───────┬───────┘
        ▼
┌───────────────┐
│ Filter 1 (Out)│  (Response logging, compression)
└───────┬───────┘
      │
      ▼
Client Response
```

---

## Filter Interface Contract

| Method | Invocation | Typical Responsibility |
| :--- | :--- | :--- |
| **`init(FilterConfig config)`** | Exactly once during container startup | Initialize resources, read init parameters (default method in Servlet 4.0+) |
| **`doFilter(req, res, chain)`** | On every intercepted request matching pattern | Inspect/modify request, call `chain.doFilter()`, modify response |
| **`destroy()`** | Exactly once during container shutdown | Release native resources, close loggers (default method) |

---

## Production Implementation: Request Timing & Security Headers Filter

```java
import jakarta.servlet.Filter;
import jakarta.servlet.FilterChain;
import jakarta.servlet.ServletException;
import jakarta.servlet.ServletRequest;
import jakarta.servlet.ServletResponse;
import jakarta.servlet.annotation.WebFilter;
import jakarta.servlet.http.HttpServletRequest;
import jakarta.servlet.http.HttpServletResponse;
import java.io.IOException;

@WebFilter(urlPatterns = "/*")
public class SecurityHeadersFilter implements Filter {

    @Override
    public void doFilter(ServletRequest req, ServletResponse res, FilterChain chain)
            throws IOException, ServletException {

        HttpServletRequest request = (HttpServletRequest) req;
        HttpServletResponse response = (HttpServletResponse) res;

        long startTime = System.currentTimeMillis();

        // Enforce baseline HTTP security headers
        response.setHeader("X-Content-Type-Options", "nosniff");
        response.setHeader("X-Frame-Options", "DENY");
        response.setHeader("Content-Security-Policy", "default-src 'self'");

        try {
            // Pass execution to next filter in the chain or target servlet
            chain.doFilter(request, response);
        } finally {
            long duration = System.currentTimeMillis() - startTime;
            System.out.printf("[AUDIT] %s %s - %d ms%n", request.getMethod(), request.getRequestURI(), duration);
        }
    }
}
```

---

## Common Pitfalls & Runtime Traps

- **Omitting `chain.doFilter()`:**
  Failing to invoke `chain.doFilter(request, response)` halts request processing entirely. The client receives an empty 200 OK response or hangs without reaching the target servlet. Only omit `chain.doFilter()` when deliberately rejecting unauthorized requests (e.g., returning `401 Unauthorized`).
- **Ordering Ambiguity with `@WebFilter`:**
  The `@WebFilter` annotation does not provide an ordering attribute. When multiple filters intercept the same URL, the container's execution order is non-deterministic. To enforce strict deterministic ordering (e.g., AuthFilter before LoggingFilter), declare the filters in `web.xml` using `<filter-mapping>` tags.
- **Reading the Request Body Multiple Times:**
  Calling `request.getReader()` inside a filter consumes the input stream. Subsequent filters or the target servlet will read an empty stream. To inspect request payloads in filters, wrap the request using `HttpServletRequestWrapper` with a cached byte buffer.

---

## Verification & Practice

**Question:** An engineer wants to log the exact request payload (JSON) in an audit filter before the servlet processes it. Why does reading `request.getReader()` in the filter break the downstream servlet?

**Explanation:** The `ServletInputStream` is a forward-only, one-time readable stream. Once consumed by the filter's `getReader()`, the stream reaches EOF. When the servlet later calls `req.getReader()`, it finds an empty stream. The solution is to extend `HttpServletRequestWrapper`, read the stream into a byte array once, and return a fresh stream on each subsequent `getInputStream()` / `getReader()` call.

## Overview
Web Listeners implement the Observer pattern to receive container notifications when key lifecycle events occur across an application's lifecycle. Listeners monitor the creation, destruction, and attribute modifications of the `ServletContext` (application scope), `HttpSession` (session scope), and `ServletRequest` (request scope).

---

## Listener Classification Hierarchy

| Interface | Scope Monitored | Triggering Event | Typical Use Case |
| :--- | :--- | :--- | :--- |
| **`ServletContextListener`** | Application | Container startup & shutdown | Initialize connection pools, load global caches |
| **`ServletContextAttributeListener`** | Application | `setAttribute()`, `removeAttribute()` | Audit global configuration changes |
| **`HttpSessionListener`** | User Session | Session created & destroyed/timed out | Track active concurrent user sessions |
| **`HttpSessionAttributeListener`** | User Session | Session attribute added, replaced, removed| Track login events, replicate state |
| **`ServletRequestListener`** | Single Request | Request enters & leaves container | Attach correlation IDs, track request latency |
| **`ServletRequestAttributeListener`**| Single Request | Request attribute modifications | Request pipeline debugging |

---

## Production Implementation: Application Lifecycle & Active Session Monitor

```java
import jakarta.servlet.ServletContextEvent;
import jakarta.servlet.ServletContextListener;
import jakarta.servlet.annotation.WebListener;
import jakarta.servlet.http.HttpSessionEvent;
import jakarta.servlet.http.HttpSessionListener;
import java.util.concurrent.atomic.AtomicInteger;

@WebListener
public class AppMonitoringListener implements ServletContextListener, HttpSessionListener {

    private final AtomicInteger activeSessions = new AtomicInteger(0);

    @Override
    public void contextInitialized(ServletContextEvent sce) {
        sce.getServletContext().log("[INIT] Application context initialized. Pre-warming connection pools...");
        sce.getServletContext().setAttribute("activeSessionCounter", activeSessions);
    }

    @Override
    public void contextDestroyed(ServletContextEvent sce) {
        sce.getServletContext().log("[SHUTDOWN] Application context shutting down. Flushing pools...");
    }

    @Override
    public void sessionCreated(HttpSessionEvent se) {
        int current = activeSessions.incrementAndGet();
        System.out.printf("[SESSION CREATED] ID: %s | Active Users: %d%n", se.getSession().getId(), current);
    }

    @Override
    public void sessionDestroyed(HttpSessionEvent se) {
        int current = activeSessions.decrementAndGet();
        System.out.printf("[SESSION EXPIRED] ID: %s | Active Users: %d%n", se.getSession().getId(), current);
    }
}
```

---

## Common Pitfalls & Lifecycle Traps

- **Throwing Exceptions in `contextInitialized`:**
  If `ServletContextListener.contextInitialized()` throws an unhandled runtime exception, the container immediately aborts the deployment and marks the web application as failed. Ensure external resource failures are handled or explicitly logged.
- **Accessing Session Attributes During `sessionDestroyed`:**
  Inside `sessionDestroyed()`, the session is being invalidated. Attempting to call `session.setAttribute()` throws `IllegalStateException`. You may only read existing attributes for logging purposes.
- **Memory Leaks via ThreadLocals in `ServletRequestListener`:**
  Attaching objects to `ThreadLocal` variables in `requestInitialized()` without calling `ThreadLocal.remove()` in `requestDestroyed()` leaks memory because container worker threads are pooled and reused across requests.

---

## Verification & Practice

**Question:** Which web listener is the appropriate place to initialize a shared HikariCP database connection pool for a Jakarta EE application, and why?

**Explanation:** `ServletContextListener` is the correct choice. Its `contextInitialized()` method runs exactly once when the web application is loaded by the container before any servlets or filters service incoming requests. Its `contextDestroyed()` method provides the guarantee to cleanly shut down the pool when the application is undeployed.

## Overview
The Model-View-Controller (MVC) pattern separates an enterprise web application into three distinct architectural tiers: the Controller (`HttpServlet`) handles HTTP input and flow control, the Model (domain entities and service DAOs) encapsulates business logic and data persistence, and the View (JSP templates with EL/JSTL) renders output representation.

---

## Architectural Flow: Jakarta EE MVC

```
Browser                 Controller (Servlet)        Model (Service/DAO)       View (JSP)
   │                             │                           │                     │
   │─── 1. HTTP Request ────────>│                           │                     │
   │    (/products?cat=books)    │── 2. Invoke Business Logic│                     │
   │                             │      getProducts("books")>│                     │
   │                             │<── 3. Return Model Data ──│                     │
   │                             │       List<Product>       │                     │
   │                             │                                                 │
   │                             │── 4. Bind to Request Scope: req.setAttribute()  │
   │                             │── 5. RequestDispatcher.forward() ──────────────>│
   │                             │                                                 │ Evaluates EL
   │<── 6. Rendered HTML Response──────────────────────────────────────────────────│
```

---

## Tier Responsibilities Matrix

| Component | Technology | Allowed Operations | Strictly Forbidden |
| :--- | :--- | :--- | :--- |
| **Model** | Plain Java Classes, POJOs, Records, DAOs | Database access, validation logic, entity persistence | Direct HTTP imports (`HttpServletRequest`, HTML generation) |
| **View** | JSP with EL & JSTL Core tags | Reading attributes, formatting dates/currencies, loops | Java code scriptlets (`<% %>`), JDBC queries, business logic |
| **Controller**| `HttpServlet` / Filter | Parse input params, call services, select view, forward/redirect | Generating raw HTML strings via `PrintWriter`, business logic |

---

## Production Implementation: Controller & Model Binding

```java
package com.example.controller;

import jakarta.servlet.ServletException;
import jakarta.servlet.annotation.WebServlet;
import jakarta.servlet.http.HttpServlet;
import jakarta.servlet.http.HttpServletRequest;
import jakarta.servlet.http.HttpServletResponse;
import java.io.IOException;
import java.util.List;

// Model Record
record AccountSummary(String accountId, String holderName, double balance) {}

@WebServlet("/account/details")
public class AccountController extends HttpServlet {

    @Override
    protected void doGet(HttpServletRequest req, HttpServletResponse resp) throws ServletException, IOException {
        String accountId = req.getParameter("id");

        if (accountId == null || accountId.isBlank()) {
            resp.sendError(HttpServletResponse.SC_BAD_REQUEST, "Missing account identifier.");
            return;
        }

        // Delegate to Service/Model tier
        AccountSummary account = new AccountSummary(accountId, "Enterprise Corp", 45892.50);

        // Bind model to request scope
        req.setAttribute("account", account);

        // Forward to View located behind protected WEB-INF boundary
        req.getRequestDispatcher("/WEB-INF/views/account-details.jsp").forward(req, resp);
    }
}
```

---

## Common Pitfalls & Architectural Antipatterns

- **Model-in-View Leakage:**
  Executing database queries directly inside JSP files via scriptlets violates MVC. It bypasses transaction boundaries, prevents connection pool cleanup on exceptions, and makes automated testing impossible.
- **Bypassing the Controller:**
  Placing JSP views in the public web root allows clients to access them directly via URL (`/views/account.jsp`), bypassing the Controller. The view loads without required request attributes, resulting in broken pages or `NullPointerException`. Always store views under `WEB-INF/views/`.
- **Overloading Controllers with Business Logic:**
  Placing SQL queries and calculation algorithms directly inside `doGet()` or `doPost()` couples web transport with business logic. Controllers should only extract input, delegate to services, and select views.

---

## Verification & Practice

**Question:** Why must JSP views in an MVC application be stored under the `/WEB-INF/` directory rather than the public root of the WAR file?

**Explanation:** The servlet specification dictates that containers must reject all direct client HTTP requests to `/WEB-INF/` with a 404 or 403 status. This guarantees that views can only be rendered via internal server-side dispatching (`RequestDispatcher.forward()`) originating from a Controller servlet, ensuring business validation and model population always occur first.

## Overview
Web applications process client input through standard URL-encoded forms (`application/x-www-form-urlencoded`) and multipart form data (`multipart/form-data`). In Jakarta Servlet, file uploads are handled declaratively using the `@MultipartConfig` annotation, exposing uploaded binary streams through the `jakarta.servlet.http.Part` interface.

---

## Encoding Types Comparison

| Encoding Type | Content-Type Header | Payload Structure | Best Used For |
| :--- | :--- | :--- | :--- |
| **URL-Encoded** | `application/x-www-form-urlencoded` | Key-value pairs: `name=John+Doe&age=30` | Standard text fields and short form submissions |
| **Multipart** | `multipart/form-data; boundary=...` | MIME boundaries separating individual parts | Binary files (images, PDFs) + mixed text fields |

---

## `@MultipartConfig` Configuration Attributes

| Attribute | Type | Default Value | Description |
| :--- | :--- | :--- | :--- |
| **`location`** | `String` | `""` (temp dir) | Directory where files are buffered on disk when exceeding threshold |
| **`maxFileSize`** | `long` | `-1L` (unlimited)| Maximum permitted file size in bytes for an individual file part |
| **`maxRequestSize`**| `long` | `-1L` (unlimited)| Maximum permitted total size in bytes for the entire multipart request |
| **`fileSizeThreshold`**| `int` | `0` bytes | Memory threshold; files larger than this are written to disk buffer |

---

## Production Implementation: Secure File Upload Controller

```java
import jakarta.servlet.ServletException;
import jakarta.servlet.annotation.MultipartConfig;
import jakarta.servlet.annotation.WebServlet;
import jakarta.servlet.http.HttpServlet;
import jakarta.servlet.http.HttpServletRequest;
import jakarta.servlet.http.HttpServletResponse;
import jakarta.servlet.http.Part;
import java.io.IOException;
import java.io.InputStream;
import java.nio.file.Files;
import java.nio.file.Path;
import java.nio.file.Paths;
import java.nio.file.StandardCopyOption;
import java.util.UUID;

@WebServlet("/upload/avatar")
@MultipartConfig(
    fileSizeThreshold = 1024 * 1024,      // 1 MB memory buffer
    maxFileSize = 1024 * 1024 * 5,         // 5 MB max per file
    maxRequestSize = 1024 * 1024 * 10     // 10 MB max request total
)
public class FileUploadServlet extends HttpServlet {

    private static final Path UPLOAD_DIR = Paths.get("/var/app/uploads");

    @Override
    protected void doPost(HttpServletRequest req, HttpServletResponse resp) throws ServletException, IOException {
        Part filePart = req.getPart("avatar");

        if (filePart == null || filePart.getSize() == 0) {
            resp.sendError(HttpServletResponse.SC_BAD_REQUEST, "No file uploaded.");
            return;
        }

        // Validate MIME type
        String contentType = filePart.getContentType();
        if (contentType == null || !contentType.startsWith("image/")) {
            resp.sendError(HttpServletResponse.SC_UNSUPPORTED_MEDIA_TYPE, "Only images allowed.");
            return;
        }

        // Sanitize file name to prevent Path Traversal attacks (CVE-standard)
        String extension = contentType.equals("image/png") ? ".png" : ".jpg";
        String safeFileName = UUID.randomUUID() + extension;
        Path destination = UPLOAD_DIR.resolve(safeFileName).normalize();

        // Enforce boundary check
        if (!destination.startsWith(UPLOAD_DIR)) {
            throw new SecurityException("Path traversal attempt detected.");
        }

        try (InputStream input = filePart.getInputStream()) {
            Files.copy(input, destination, StandardCopyOption.REPLACE_EXISTING);
        }

        resp.setStatus(HttpServletResponse.SC_CREATED);
        resp.getWriter().printf("File uploaded successfully as: %s%n", safeFileName);
    }
}
```

---

## Common Pitfalls & Security Traps

- **Missing `@MultipartConfig` Annotation:**
  Attempting to call `req.getPart("file")` on a servlet without `@MultipartConfig` throws `ServletException: Unable to process parts as no multi-part configuration is provided`.
- **Path Traversal Vulnerability via `getSubmittedFileName()`:**
  `part.getSubmittedFileName()` returns the exact client-supplied filename, which may contain malicious sequences like `../../etc/passwd`. Never concatenate the submitted filename directly into a filesystem path; always generate a synthetic UUID or sanitize strictly.
- **Denial of Service via Unbounded File Uploads:**
  Omitting `maxFileSize` and `maxRequestSize` allows malicious clients to upload gigabyte-sized files, causing server disk exhaustion or `OutOfMemoryError`. Always configure explicit byte ceilings.

---

## Verification & Practice

**Question:** An attacker uploads a file named `avatar.jpg` containing raw executable PHP or JSP code into a publicly served directory. Why is generating a random `UUID` and storing files outside the web root essential?

**Explanation:** If an uploaded file retains its original name and is stored inside a publicly accessible web root, the web server or container might execute it as server-side script when requested via URL, granting the attacker Remote Code Execution (RCE). Storing files outside the web root under synthetic UUID names ensures they can never be directly executed by the server.

## Overview
Opening physical database connections involves costly TCP handshakes, TLS negotiation, and authentication exchanges. Connection pooling maintains a pool of pre-allocated, reusable `java.sql.Connection` instances. In Jakarta EE, pools are configured either as container resources looked up via Java Naming and Directory Interface (JNDI) or programmatically initialized using high-performance connection poolers like HikariCP.

---

## Connection Pooling Architecture

```
Web App Worker Threads                    HikariCP Connection Pool             Database Server
        │                                            │                                │
        │── 1. dataSource.getConnection() ──────────>│                                │
        │                                            │ Allocates idle physical conn   │
        │<── 2. Returns Connection Proxy (Wrapped) ──│ (Pre-authenticated socket)     │
        │                                            │                                │
        │── 3. Executes SQL queries ─────────────────────────────────────────────────>│
        │                                            │                                │
        │── 4. conn.close() (Invoked on Proxy) ─────>│                                │
        │                                            │ Resets connection state &      │
        │                                            │ returns to idle pool           │
```

---

## Critical Connection Pool Configuration Parameters

| Parameter (HikariCP) | Default | Recommended Value | Engineering Rationale |
| :--- | :--- | :--- | :--- |
| **`maximumPoolSize`** | 10 | $2 \times \text{CPU cores} + \text{disk spindles}$ | Prevents context-switching contention on database server |
| **`minimumIdle`** | Same as max | Same as `maximumPoolSize` | Fixed-size pool avoids connection creation latency spikes |
| **`connectionTimeout`**| 30,000 ms | 10,000 – 15,000 ms | Time thread waits for connection before throwing `SQLException` |
| **`idleTimeout`** | 600,000 ms| 300,000 ms | Time an idle connection can sit before being retired |
| **`maxLifetime`** | 1,800,000 ms| 1,200,000 ms (20 min) | Shorter than database-side wait_timeout to prevent broken sockets |

---

## Production Implementation: HikariCP DataSource Factory

```java
import com.zaxxer.hikari.HikariConfig;
import com.zaxxer.hikari.HikariDataSource;
import java.sql.Connection;
import java.sql.PreparedStatement;
import java.sql.ResultSet;
import java.sql.SQLException;
import javax.sql.DataSource;

public class DatabaseManager {

    private static final HikariDataSource dataSource;

    static {
        HikariConfig config = new HikariConfig();
        config.setJdbcUrl("jdbc:postgresql://localhost:5432/appdb");
        config.setUsername("db_user");
        config.setPassword("secure_password");
        config.setMaximumPoolSize(20);
        config.setMinimumIdle(20);
        config.setConnectionTimeout(10000);
        config.setMaxLifetime(1200000);
        config.addDataSourceProperty("cachePrepStmts", "true");
        config.addDataSourceProperty("prepStmtCacheSize", "250");
        config.addDataSourceProperty("prepStmtCacheSqlLimit", "2048");

        dataSource = new HikariDataSource(config);
    }

    public static DataSource getDataSource() {
        return dataSource;
    }

    public static void executeQuery() throws SQLException {
        String sql = "SELECT id, username FROM users WHERE active = ?";

        // Try-with-resources closes connection proxy, returning it to pool
        try (Connection conn = dataSource.getConnection();
             PreparedStatement stmt = conn.prepareStatement(sql)) {

            stmt.setBoolean(1, true);
            try (ResultSet rs = stmt.executeQuery()) {
                while (rs.next()) {
                    System.out.println("User: " + rs.getString("username"));
                }
            }
        }
    }
}
```

---

## Common Pitfalls & Production Traps

- **Connection Leaks (Failure to Close):**
  Failing to close a connection in a `finally` block or `try-with-resources` prevents the connection from returning to the pool. When all connections in `maximumPoolSize` are leaked, every subsequent request blocks until `connectionTimeout` expires, bringing down the entire application.
- **Setting `maximumPoolSize` Too High:**
  Setting pool sizes to 200+ under high concurrency overloads the database engine's CPU and disk I/O, degrading query throughput. A pool size of 20–30 connections frequently outperforms a pool size of 200 due to reduced CPU context switching.
- **`maxLifetime` Exceeding Database Timeout:**
  If the database engine (e.g., MySQL `wait_timeout`) terminates idle sockets before Hikari's `maxLifetime`, the pool hands dead sockets to application threads, throwing `CommunicationsException: Communications link failure`. Always configure `maxLifetime` a few minutes shorter than the DB timeout.

---

## Verification & Practice

**Question:** When an application calls `connection.close()` on a connection obtained from a `HikariDataSource`, does it terminate the physical TCP socket connection to the database?

**Explanation:** No. `HikariDataSource.getConnection()` returns a wrapper proxy (`HikariProxyConnection`). Calling `.close()` on the proxy intercepts the call, rolls back any uncommitted transactions, resets connection flags, and returns the underlying physical connection to the idle pool ready for the next thread.

## Overview
Jakarta Persistence (JPA) is the standard specification for Object-Relational Mapping (ORM) in Java, implemented by engines such as Hibernate. JPA bridges object-oriented domain models with relational database tables, managing entity state transitions, automated dirty checking, identity mapping, and transactional persistence via the `EntityManager` interface.

---

## Entity Lifecycle State Transitions

```
                 Entity Lifecycle States
    new Product()
          │
          ▼
    ┌───────────┐    em.persist(e)     ┌───────────┐
    │ Transient ├─────────────────────>│  Managed  │<──┐
    └───────────┘                      └─────┬─────┘   │ em.merge(e)
                                 em.remove() │         │
                                             ▼         │
                                       ┌───────────┐   │
                                       │  Removed  │   │
                                       └───────────┘   │
                                             ▲         │
                               em.detach() / │         │
                               em.close()    ▼         │
                                       ┌───────────┐   │
                                       │ Detached  ├───┘
                                       └───────────┘
```

---

## Entity States Specification Matrix

| State | Database Record Exists? | Tracked by Persistence Context? | Can Trigger Automated Dirty Checking? |
| :--- | :--- | :--- | :--- |
| **Transient** | No (new in memory) | No | No |
| **Managed** | Yes (or queued on flush) | **Yes (1st Level Cache)** | **Yes** (modifications synced to DB on commit) |
| **Detached** | Yes | No (context closed or detached)| No (must call `em.merge()`) |
| **Removed** | Scheduled for deletion | Yes | Yes (triggers SQL `DELETE` on commit) |

---

## Production Implementation: Standard JPA Entity & Persistence Logic

```java
package com.example.model;

import jakarta.persistence.Column;
import jakarta.persistence.Entity;
import jakarta.persistence.GeneratedValue;
import jakarta.persistence.GenerationType;
import jakarta.persistence.Id;
import jakarta.persistence.Table;
import jakarta.persistence.Version;
import java.math.BigDecimal;

@Entity
@Table(name = "products")
public class Product {

    @Id
    @GeneratedValue(strategy = GenerationType.IDENTITY)
    private Long id;

    @Column(nullable = false, length = 100)
    private String name;

    @Column(nullable = false, precision = 10, scale = 2)
    private BigDecimal price;

    @Version
    private Long version; // Optimistic locking protection

    public Product() {} // Required no-arg constructor

    public Product(String name, BigDecimal price) {
        this.name = name;
        this.price = price;
    }

    public void setPrice(BigDecimal price) { this.price = price; }
    public Long getId() { return id; }
    public String getName() { return name; }
    public BigDecimal getPrice() { return price; }
}
```

```java
import jakarta.persistence.EntityManager;
import jakarta.persistence.EntityManagerFactory;
import jakarta.persistence.Persistence;
import java.math.BigDecimal;

public class ProductDao {
    private static final EntityManagerFactory emf =
            Persistence.createEntityManagerFactory("production-pu");

    public void updatePrice(Long productId, BigDecimal newPrice) {
        EntityManager em = emf.createEntityManager();
        try {
            em.getTransaction().begin();

            // Product enters MANAGED state
            Product product = em.find(Product.class, productId);
            if (product != null) {
                // Automated Dirty Checking: modifying setter triggers SQL UPDATE on commit
                product.setPrice(newPrice);
            }

            em.getTransaction().commit(); // Flushes changes to DB
        } catch (Exception e) {
            if (em.getTransaction().isActive()) em.getTransaction().rollback();
            throw e;
        } finally {
            em.close(); // Product transitions to DETACHED state
        }
    }
}
```

